PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Key facts: cholesterol required for ecdysone (molting hormone) + structural roles; cholestanol only structural (cannot be converted to ecdysone). 250 mg/L chol supports full development; 2 mg/L or 0 mg/L does not; 250 mg/L cholestanol yields zero adult survival. Maternal provisioning deposits sterol reserve in eggs when mothers on cholesterol diet (none when mothers on cholestanol). 

Round-0 split: C (Death, No eclosion) favored by those arguing maternal reserve too small to reach pupation/eclosion on pure cholestanol, so early lethality; H (No eclosion, Death) favored by those arguing reserve suffices for larval/pupal stages but exhausts before final ecdysone peak needed for eclosion, while scenario 2 has zero reserve + sub-threshold cholesterol so outright larval death. Minor votes for B/G (both scenarios insufficient) or F/D (cholestanol + trace chol fully rescues). 

My synthesis: H best reconciles dose thresholds, explicit zero adult survival on cholestanol, and maternal carry-over effect; C underestimates how far a one-generation reserve can carry development. No consensus yet; need to track which outcome descriptions match “death before pupation” vs “failure at eclosion.”}

